forti-cli (0.4.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Depend on valkyrianlabs-archive-keyring so renewed APT signing-key metadata
    reaches existing installs through upgrades (it owns
    /usr/share/keyrings/valkyrianlabs.gpg and links an unmodified
    /etc/apt/keyrings/valkyrianlabs.gpg to it).
  * New `forti history`: the audit log for people (newest last; `-p` filters by
    profile, `-n` limits, `--all` shows refused attempts, `--verbose` adds
    operations, approvals and the undo command; `--json` prints the events as
    recorded). Reads only the state directory.
  * New `forti completion bash|zsh|fish`; the scripts are installed to
    `/usr/share/bash-completion/completions/forti`,
    `/usr/share/zsh/site-functions/_forti` and
    `/usr/share/fish/vendor_completions.d/forti.fish` (Meson option
    `completions`). Candidates come from the hidden `forti __complete` (profile
    names, resource paths, pending plan ids; no device access, no secrets).
  * New `forti set|create|delete`: one command per change, at most one
    question. read-only shows the change and the mode command; guarded asks
    "Apply these changes? [y/N]" at a terminal and otherwise stops at the
    approval (a missing terminal is never a yes); autonomous applies. Resources
    are named as for `forti get`; `--dry-run`, `--verbose`, `--json`.
  * New `forti approve [id] [--yes]` and `forti approve --watch`: approve a
    plan for the agent that made it. Without a terminal only `--yes` approves
    (previously stdout not being a terminal was enough). `forti plan approve`
    is the same command; it no longer offers "Apply now?".
  * Plans are picked without an id only when one is waiting for the profile
    (made in the last day); at a terminal several are listed to choose from.
    Without a terminal apply/approve need the id.
  * `forti apply`, `forti rollback` and `forti plan --apply` show the change
    concisely and ask once.
  * Engine: approvals are bound to the digest of the plan shown
    (`approve_plan(..., shown_digest)`); audit events record the actor
    (`cli:USER`, `mcp:CLIENT`), a summary and who approved the plan; the
    approval command is `forti approve ID`.
  * The forti workspace denies Claude Code's shell `forti approve`,
    `forti plan approve` and `forti profile mode`.
  * `forti configure`: Enter on the empty API key prompt prints the FortiGate
    steps (no separate question); a second profile for the same FortiGate
    (serial or address) is noted; profiles record the serial the check saw.
  * 401 advice: the key may be right; FortiOS also answers 401 outside the API
    user's trusted hosts. 403 points at the admin profile.
  * Build: hidden symbol visibility: `forti` starts in ~6 ms again (forti-api's
    inline schema tables were coalesced by dyld at every start, ~20 ms).
  * Release: the clean-install smoke test adds the ValkyrianLabs repository
    (the keyring dependency). 0.4.0 was tagged but not published.

 -- Cooper Larson <cooper@valkyrianlabs.com>  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:32:38 +0000

forti-cli (0.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * configure: one address prompt (host[:port] or https URL, also --host);
    trust on first use via forti-api probe_tls (show, confirm, pin the device
    certificate as ca_bundle; serial-named certificates via resolve_ip); API
    key as typed / env:VAR / cmd:CMD; FortiOS commands for a REST API admin;
    VDOM / CA bundle / resolve-ip only on need or --advanced; name after the
    device; recovery menu that keeps answers; --like PROFILE; first scripted
    profile defaults to "default".
  * cert: named sets in <home>/certs/<name>/, issued by the forti CA in
    <home>/ca/ unless --own-ca; configure offers reuse/create (--cert NAME);
    new cert list and cert use; FortiOS steps carry the CA inline.
  * plan/apply: id prefixes, last part and latest; newest pending plan at a
    terminal; inline approval for guarded apply; Apply now? after approve;
    --apply; new rollback command; plan create attr=value; attribute
    suggestions; digest only with --verbose.
  * get/discover: CLI word order, unique leaf names, keys in the path, --all;
    paging notes on stderr; attribute suggestions.
  * mcp: `forti mcp install` (install mcp kept), Yes defaults for apply and
    Codex trust, one probe per client, ~ paths; workspace denies <home>/ca/.
  * doctor: certificate expiry (30 days), the fix for a missing certificate, ~
    paths, summary after the report. Errors list profiles; missing certificate
    files are caught before any request.
  * Packaging: Homebrew bottle built and published with each release; the
    formula depends on forti-mcp and the .deb recommends it.
  * plans: created_ns orders plans made within one second (`latest`, plan
    list); 0.3.0 was tagged but not published.

 -- Cooper Larson <cooper@valkyrianlabs.com>  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 17:34:43 +0000

forti-cli (0.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Home, certificates and profiles
    - One forti home: `$FORTI_HOME`, else `~/.forti` (0700), for config.json,
      credentials/, state/, cache/ and certs/. `FORTI_CONFIG_DIR`,
      `FORTI_STATE_DIR`, `FORTI_CACHE_DIR` still override. A pre-0.2 XDG setup
      (`~/.config/forti`, `~/.local/state/forti`, `~/.cache/forti`) stays in
      use until migrated.
    - New `forti home` (every location, legacy flag) and `forti home migrate`
      (renames config.json, credentials/, state and cache into the home; modes
      kept; config.json last; idempotent; never overwrites a home config.json;
      refuses while a `FORTI_*_DIR` override is set).
    - `forti doctor` has a first check, `home`, that warns on a pre-0.2 setup.
    - New `forti cert create [--profile] [--days N] [--force] [--keychain]`: a
      private CA and a client certificate (CN `forti-<profile>`, RSA 3072,
      SHA-256) in `<home>/certs/<profile>/` through the `openssl` command
      (argv, no shell). The PKCS#12 uses PBE-SHA1-3DES with a SHA-1 MAC
      (OpenSSL 3 without the legacy provider, LibreSSL and FortiOS read it).
      Sets the profile's client_cert and certificate password, prints the
      password-reading command (never the password) and the FortiOS steps
      (`config vpn certificate ca`, `config user peer`, `config user peergrp`,
      `config system api-user` `peer-auth`/`peer-group`). Plus
      `forti cert show` and `forti cert remove`.
    - New SecretRef source `file` (absolute path; owner-only regular file, as
      the file store requires); `forti configure --cert-password-file PATH`.
    - New `forti profile set NAME key=value...` (validated as config.json is;
      re-binds stored secrets when host or port change),
      `forti profile rename OLD NEW` (moves stored secrets, `certs/OLD`,
      references and the default) and `forti profile copy SOURCE NEW` (settings
      only, no secrets).
    - `forti configure` on an existing profile prefills the API key command and
      keeps the current certificate password source on Enter.
    - Debian: `forti-cli` depends on `openssl`.
    - `forti install mcp --scope workspace`: register forti-mcp as project
      scope in the forti home (~/.forti or $FORTI_HOME), the recommended
      default and first interactive choice
      + scaffold, created and updated idempotently, never overwriting an edited
        file: `.mcp.json`, `.codex/config.toml`, the skill in `.claude/skills`
        and `.agents/skills`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` (`@AGENTS.md`), and
        `.forti-workspace.json` recording what forti wrote
      + secret hygiene: `.claude/settings.json` gains `permissions.deny` rules
        (`Read(/credentials/**)`, `Read(/certs/**/*.pass)`,
        `Read(/certs/**/*.p12)`, `Read(/certs/**/ca/**)`,
        `Edit(/config.json)`), merged into an existing file; Codex gets
        `default_permissions = "forti-workspace"`, a permission profile that
        denies the same paths and makes config.json read-only for sandboxed
        commands
      + Codex trust for the forti home only with consent (asked interactively,
        default no; or `--trust-codex-workspace`): exactly
        `[projects."<home>"] trust_level = "trusted"` is added to the global
        config with a format-preserving edit, and removed on uninstall only if
        forti added it
    - Home directory guard: project or local scope with $HOME as the project
      warns and asks "Are you sure?" (default no); without a terminal it is
      refused unless `--force`
    - `forti mcp status` shows the workspace scope and scaffold health (JSON:
      `workspace`); `forti mcp uninstall --scope workspace` removes the
      registration and forti's scaffold files, never config.json, credentials,
      certs or state
    - Skill: mentions the workspace convention and that the forti home's
      secrets are off limits

